.TH MDNS 8 "October 2026" "mdns 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mdns \- mDNS discovery and reflection daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdns
[\fIconfig-file\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdns
tracks the devices announcing themselves with multicast DNS (Bonjour, Avahi)
on a set of network interfaces, and reflects them between the interfaces you
choose, and only those.
Given
.B br0 <-> media
and
.BR "br0 <-> wifi" ,
the
.B media
and
.B wifi
networks never learn about each other's devices, even though both talk to
.BR br0 .
.PP
For each interface and each address family (IPv4 and IPv6, which are kept
completely separate) the daemon opens one socket, bound to that interface and
to the mDNS multicast group address.
It never listens on the wildcard address.
.PP
What may be reflected where is described by the
.B [Segment]
sections of the configuration file; see
.BR mdnsd.conf (5).
.SS Tracking
A device is known by its hostname once it has announced one, and until then by
the address it was heard from.
Its addresses are those of its own address records (and the address it sent
from, if it announced none of its own); per address family it has up to two, a
link-local one and a routable one.
Its DNS-SD services are taken from the records it announces.
A packet may describe several devices, as a reflector's answer to a browse
query does, so each record is attributed to the device it names: an address
record to the host that owns the name, and a service to the host its SRV record
points at, together with the PTR and TXT records of that instance.
The address a packet came from is credited to a host only if that host
announced it, or announced no address at all and is the only host described;
a reflector announcing other devices is not mistaken for any of them.
A device expires after it has been silent for at least the minimum lifetime
(five minutes by default, see
.B MinimumLifetime=
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)),
or for the TTL of its records if that is longer.
A goodbye packet (TTL 0) removes it immediately.
.SS Probing
Devices announce themselves rarely, and some only answer when asked, so waiting
is not enough.
The daemon therefore also asks.
A probe is three rounds of multicast queries, a few seconds in all: the first
asks which service types exist; the next browses every type learned so far for
instances; and instances that have not yet said where they are (their target
and port) are resolved by asking for their service and text records; and a
device that has a name but no routable address yet, because it only ever showed
a link-local one, is asked for all of its addresses.
A device needs a hostname, a routable address and a service to be reflected, and
the resolve steps are what supply the hostname and the address for devices that
only answer browse queries.
.PP
A probe runs on every interface at startup, and again whenever
.B mdnsctl probe
is run, or every
.B ProbeInterval=
if one is set for a segment the interface is in, or in the
.B [Probe]
section of
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)
(it is not by default).
.PP
Most devices only answer when asked, and one that is not heard from expires, so
a device that is about to expire is asked to answer again, on its own: this long
before the end of its lifetime, which is the longest TTL it announced and at
least the minimum lifetime, the daemon asks that device, and only it, for the records it
announced, and asks again until it answers or expires.
This is the
.B RefreshBefore=
setting of the
.B [Probe]
section (30 seconds by default); see
.BR mdnsd.conf (5).
.SS Advertising
With
.B Hostname=
in the
.B [Advertise]
section of
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
the daemon also announces and answers for its own name, for example
.BR router.local ,
the way Avahi does for the host it runs on.
On each interface it publishes the address of this host on that interface (an
A record with the primary IPv4 address, an AAAA record with the link-local
IPv6 address), read once at startup.
The name is handled exactly like a reflected device that has no services: it
is announced, re-announced at 80% of its TTL (see
.B TTL=
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)),
answers queries, and is withdrawn
with goodbye packets on
.BR SIGTERM .
No conflict detection is done: the name is assumed to be yours alone.
.SS Reflection
Within a segment, every interface announces the devices discovered on every
other interface of that segment.
Only devices that announced a hostname, a routable address and at least one
service are reflected; chatty clients such as phones are tracked but not
reflected.
A link-local address is never reflected: it only means something on the link
the device is on.
A device is announced with its routable address only, so one that has only a
link-local IPv6 address is not reflected over IPv6.
Reflection is never transitive: a device reflected onto an interface is not
reflected again.
.PP
Reflected devices are announced with their routable address, so the firewall
must allow the actual connections between the networks; mDNS only makes the
device discoverable.
.PP
The daemon answers queries for reflected devices, including known-answer
suppression, keeps them alive with re-announcements at 80% of the shortest TTL they
announced (see
.B ReflectTTL=
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5)),
and withdraws
them with goodbye packets when the source device disappears.
An interface listed in
.B Untrusted=
is announced nothing by its segment, though its own devices are announced to the
others, so a network can be seen from another without seeing it back.
If devices on different interfaces announce the same hostname, the one from the
interface listed first in the segment wins, and where segments share an
interface, the one from the segment listed first in the config; the other takes
over when it goes.
The advertised name always wins over a reflected device.
A device on the interface itself comes first of all: while it has a hostname,
or a service instance name, no reflected copy of it is announced there.
Different hostnames never clash, even on one address: a device with several
names has each of them reflected.
.SS Control socket
The daemon serves a local control socket, described under
.B [Ipc]
in
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
which
.BR mdnsctl (1)
uses to list segments, devices and timers, and to follow devices as they appear and go.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I config-file
Read the configuration from this file instead of
.IR /etc/mdnsd.conf .
The configuration is read once at startup; restart the daemon to apply changes.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Send goodbye packets for every reflected device, remove the control socket and
exit with status 0, so that clients drop the reflected devices immediately.
.PP
If the daemon is killed with
.B SIGKILL
or crashes, no goodbyes are sent, and clients keep the reflected devices until
their records expire, up to 75 minutes.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Stopped by
.B SIGTERM
or
.BR SIGINT .
.TP
.B 1
Startup failed: the configuration is invalid, an interface cannot be opened, or
the control socket cannot be created.
The reason is printed on standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock
Default control socket.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/mdnsd.service
systemd unit.
It runs the daemon as the unprivileged
.B mdnsd
user, starts it only once
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
exists, and restarts it every five seconds if it exits, which covers interfaces
that appear after boot.
.SH NOTES
Binding a socket to a single interface needs no privilege on Linux 5.7 and
newer.
.PP
The daemon binds with
.BR SO_REUSEPORT ,
so it can run next to Avahi while migrating, but do not run two reflectors
(this one and Avahi's) over the same interfaces.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run in the foreground with a configuration file of your own:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ mdns ./mdnsd.conf
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run as a service:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# cp /usr/share/doc/mdns/examples/mdnsd.conf /etc/mdnsd.conf
# $EDITOR /etc/mdnsd.conf
# systemctl enable \-\-now mdnsd.service
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdnsd.conf (5),
.BR mdnsctl (1)
